1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:07,400
The Untapped Therapeutic Frontier There are nine point five million possible drug-disease combinations.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,760
You've tested thirty-two thousand five hundred. That's like searching for your keys in zero point three four percent of

3
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:22,740
your house and declaring them lost. The other ninety-nine point seven percent is sitting right there, untested, because

4
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:30,660
paperwork. You think you've explored medicine. In reality, you're standing in the parking lot of Disney World bragging about

5
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:34,680
how fun the pavement is. You've taken pictures of the asphalt.

6
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,640
You've written dissertations about the parking stripes. You've never gone inside.

7
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:46,570
After a century of pharmacology, most beneficial effects of molecules on diseases remain unexplored.

8
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:55,480
Not because biology is constrained. Because clinical trials are slow, expensive, and run by people who think "urgency" is

9
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:02,580
a type of perfume. Here's the math. It's painful but brief, like a flu shot administered by someone who hates you.

10
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:12,520
Your Tiny Sandbox. Here is everything humanity considers "medicine" after five thousand years of civilization: Prescriptions:

11
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:19,380
twenty thousand products approved in the U.S. Sounds impressive until you realize most are just "Tylenol, but blue.".

12
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:25,160
Unique Ingredients: These products contain only one thousand six hundred and fifty compounds.

13
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:34,180
Fewer ingredients than a decent French bakery uses. Investigational Drugs: seven thousand five hundred compounds have passed

14
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:42,880
Phase One globally. Progress! Safe Stuff: The F.D.A. generally recognized as safe, or G.R.A.S.

15
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:49,070
list contains six hundred and thirty-five substances. These are things you could eat right now without dying.

16
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:57,860
Low bar. Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

17
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:02,580
That's it. That's your entire medicine cabinet after five millennia.

18
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:11,360
The Target List (Ways Your Body Breaks). Codes: The International Classification of Diseases, or I.C.D.

19
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,260
ten lists fourteen thousand ways your meat can malfunction.

20
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,840
Humanity's most comprehensive to-do list, and you're ignoring it.

21
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:28,620
Real Targets: After consolidation, one thousand trial-relevant diseases worth actually fixing.

22
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,730
The Math You're Ignoring. Take your nine thousand five hundred compounds safe compounds.

23
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:41,600
Multiply by one thousand diseases. This gives you the "Combinatorial Space," which is fancy talk for "stuff you could test

24
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:49,000
tomorrow if you weren't busy filling out forms.". nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals

25
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,300
nine point five million combinations plausible drug-condition combinations.

26
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:02,100
What You've Actually Tested. Approved uses: one thousand seven hundred and fifty pairings unique approved drug-disease pairings.

27
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:10,900
Your entire medical canon. Repurposed uses: thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial approval.

28
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:19,540
The rest never get a second date. Failed trials: Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things that failed.

29
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:27,300
(You did. You learned nothing from them.). Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

30
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:37,520
The Scorecard. thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested divided by nine point five million combinations possibilities

31
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:47,600
equals zero point three four two percent where: Humanity has explored about a third of one percent of the theoretically

32
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:54,000
testable drug-disease space. You are the detective who searched one drawer and declared the case cold.

33
00:03:54,660 --> 00:04:00,340
It's like reading the first page of *War and Peace* and writing a book report called "It's About a Party."

34
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:06,680
Technically accurate. Cosmically insufficient. Is The Untested Stuff Useful?

35
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:12,500
You might argue, "Maybe the other ninety-nine point seven percent is useless!"

36
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:21,519
Biology would like a word. Undrugged Targets: Mapping more than three hundred and fifty thousand clinical trials showed

37
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:26,580
that only twelve percent of the human interactome has ever been targeted by drugs.

38
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:34,620
You're ignoring eighty-eight percent of your own biology. It's like having a one hundred-room mansion and living exclusively

39
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:41,720
in the bathroom. Polypharmacology: Drugs are messy. They hit multiple targets.

40
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,460
F.D.A.-approved drugs often bind to things you didn't intend.

41
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:53,800
This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else" that nobody bothered to notice.

42
00:04:53,820 --> 00:05:02,560
Sildenafil was developed for chest pain. It failed. Its "side effect" became Viagra, then got repurposed *again* for pulmonary

43
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,800
hypertension. Minoxidil was developed for high blood pressure.

44
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:12,340
Its "side effect" was growing hair. It became Rogaine. Two drugs.

45
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:20,210
Three accidental cures. Zero systematic searches. You keep finding cures by tripping over them in hallways.

46
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:28,920
Imagine if you opened a door on purpose. Network Overlap: Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

47
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:38,090
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share biological pathways. Lithium was prescribed for gout in the eighteen hundreds; decades later,

48
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:42,080
it turned out to be the gold standard treatment for bipolar disorder.

49
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:47,240
Nobody connected the dots because nobody was drawing lines between diseases.

50
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:53,700
The drug that treats your grandfather's inflammation might fix your grandmother's neurodegeneration.

51
00:05:54,240 --> 00:06:00,000
You won't find out, because testing it would require paperwork that takes longer than Grandma has left.

52
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:08,720
The Universe is Big. You Are Small. So far we only counted molecules humanity *already has*.

53
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:13,660
The stuff in your cupboards. If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets embarrassing.

54
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:21,720
Chemical Space: Estimated ten to the twenty-third to ten to the sixtieth drug-like molecules.

55
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:26,600
That's more candidates than there are atoms in your body. Or stars in the galaxy.

56
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:37,420
Or excuses from your regulatory agencies. Virtual Libraries: Zinc Is Not Commercial, or Z.I.N.C.-twenty-two, contains tens

57
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:42,680
of billions of purchasable virtual molecules. You could order them online.

58
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:50,860
You haven't. Synthesized: You have made less than ten to the negative fortieth of possible molecules.

59
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:59,320
Tested divided by possible is approximately ten to the negative twentieth to ten to the negative fiftieth.

60
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:08,180
Effectively zero. You haven't even started. This is like exploring Earth by looking at one grain of sand on one beach in

61
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:12,020
New Jersey, then declaring you've "seen the world." You haven't.

62
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:18,350
You've seen a grain of sand. In New Jersey. Why You Are Still Sick.

63
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:24,920
It's not because science is hard. It's because you've built a system that is allergic to answers.

64
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:33,720
Trials Cost Too Much: A typical Phase Two-Three randomized controlled trial, or R.C.T., costs thirty to one hundred million

65
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:37,220
dollars (Median cost per patient forty-one thousand dollars).

66
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:42,980
At that price, you only test "sure things." Safe bets. Boring bets.

67
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:50,800
Bets that don't cure cancer. Clinical trials are a velvet-rope nightclub where the bouncer is a regulatory agency and the

68
00:07:50,800 --> 00:08:00,160
cover charge is a hundred million dollars. Herd Mentality: Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due

69
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:06,700
to preferential attachment dynamics. Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

70
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:13,560
It's like if every explorer only went to France because France already had good reviews on TripAdvisor.

71
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:18,600
Meanwhile, undiscovered continents full of cures sit there, undiscovered.

72
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:27,200
No Profit: Most molecules have no patent. No patent equals no one hundred million dollar trial equals no approval.

73
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:35,380
Metformin, a diabetes drug from the nineteen fifties, shows persistent signals for reducing cancer risk and extending lifespan.

74
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:44,640
It costs pennies. It's off-patent. The trial to prove it works for aging (T.A.M.E.) spent years begging for funding because

75
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:48,620
no pharmaceutical company will bankroll a drug they can't monopolize.

76
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:54,200
The molecule that saves your life has to make money first. If it can't, it won't.

77
00:08:54,980 --> 00:09:02,960
You die. Quarterly earnings go up. Neglect: If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

78
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:10,700
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against quarterly

79
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:15,500
earnings and found you wanting. Sorry about your death. It was a business decision.

80
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:23,560
How Long to Explore Everything? At the current pace of three thousand three hundred trials per year, how long would it take

81
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:30,460
to systematically test therapeutic possibilities? Tier 1: Single Compounds (Most Conservative).

82
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:39,980
Testing every known safe compound against every major disease: Nine point five million combinations divided by three thousand

83
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:45,600
three hundred trials per year equals two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

84
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:51,960
That's almost two thousand eight hundred and eighty years to explore compounds we *already know are safe*.

85
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,920
By that math, we won't finish until the year five thousand.

86
00:09:56,820 --> 00:10:04,760
Tier 2: Combination Therapies (Still Defensible). Modern medicine increasingly relies on multi-drug cocktails.

87
00:10:04,780 --> 00:10:10,840
Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely use drug combinations.

88
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:16,780
So the real question isn't "does this one drug work?" It's "which combinations work?"

89
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:25,480
Testing pairwise combinations of safe compounds: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three thousand three

90
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:30,480
hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

91
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:34,580
That's longer than *Homo sapiens* has existed as a species.

92
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:40,240
And that's using only compounds with established safety data, testing only pairs.

93
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:47,980
Not triplets. God help you if you want to try triplets. Tier 3: The Full Chemical Universe (Illustrative).

94
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:56,760
Beyond known compounds lies the chemical space of ten to the twenty-third to ten to the sixtieth drug-like molecules.

95
00:10:57,220 --> 00:11:04,510
At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction would take longer than the universe has existed.

96
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:10,520
The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old. You need more time than that.

97
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:17,820
To test pills. Pills that already exist. We haven't even started.

98
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:25,820
But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries? Some will argue "diminishing returns": the more we search, the harder each new

99
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:30,600
discovery becomes. This assumes we've already picked the low-hanging fruit.

100
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:36,820
We haven't picked *any* fruit. We're still in the parking lot arguing about whether fruit exists.

101
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:43,040
Diminishing returns only kicks in after you've explored a significant fraction of the space.

102
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:51,320
At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new about human biology, improving your ability to

103
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:56,860
predict what works next. Learning compounds. Depletion is negligible.

104
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:02,220
You are nowhere near the bottom of the barrel because you have not yet located the barrel.

105
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:11,300
The math: even with weak learning, diminishing returns only matters after exploring roughly thirty-seven percent of therapeutic

106
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:20,560
space. For combination therapies, that crossover is over five million years away at current pace (thirty-seven percent of

107
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:26,480
forty-five point one billion combinations at three thousand three hundred trials per year).

108
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:31,640
You cannot have diminishing returns when you've barely started looking.

109
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:41,240
The Fix. The Oxford RECOVERY Trial (COVID-nineteen) proved that large-scale pragmatic trials can run for roughly five hundred

110
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:49,700
dollars and deliver results in less than one hundred days. Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars.

111
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:54,500
Read those numbers again. One of them is the reason people are dying.

112
00:12:55,320 --> 00:13:04,000
With a decentralized F.D.A., trial capacity increases by twelve point three times: Nine point five million combinations

113
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:11,200
divided by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year equals two hundred thirty-four years.

114
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:16,780
Still a long time, but at least it's a number with fewer digits than a phone book.

115
00:13:17,540 --> 00:13:24,500
And the first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries (the low-hanging fruit that current trial scarcity

116
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:28,300
forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room).

117
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:37,080
More importantly, your decentralized F.D.A. builds *permanent infrastructure* for systematic therapeutic exploration.

118
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:45,060
The goal isn't to "finish." It's to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether to form

119
00:13:45,060 --> 00:13:50,000
committees to review the proposal to consider them. The Bottom Line.

120
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:58,060
Humanity has empirically tested less than zero point three four two percent of the drug-disease relationships that can be

121
00:13:58,060 --> 00:14:06,960
tested using existing safe compounds. If you include the larger chemical universe, humanity has explored roughly one-quadrillionth

122
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:14,840
to one-quintillionth of what is possible. There are numbers so small that mathematicians get depressed looking at them.

123
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:21,760
These are those numbers. The overwhelming majority of therapeutic potential remains untouched.

124
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:27,900
Not because cures are impossible. Because trials are too expensive and too slow.

125
00:14:28,420 --> 00:14:35,900
A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine

126
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:43,520
point nine nine nine percent of medical space. You're not in the parking lot because there's nothing inside Disney World.

127
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:51,360
You're in the parking lot because the entrance fee is one hundred million dollars and requires seventeen years of paperwork.

128
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:56,060
People are dying in the parking lot. The rides are right there.

129
00:14:56,300 --> 00:15:00,580
You can hear the screaming. (Fun screaming. Cure screaming.).

130
00:15:01,300 --> 00:15:04,460
The cures are inside. Open the gate.
